Artemis the Ranger was touched by the minds of the terrible Old Ones, and 
imparted unto us these blasphemous ravings:

> The other day I was thinking about voluntary conducts. What about a
> pragmatist conduct? The definition of a pragmatist would be a person
> who does not believe in magic, in any way, shape, or form. As an
> illustration, imagine the most hard-headed of scientists going through
> the dungeon, thoroughly convinced that everything he/she experiences
> can be explained by the laws of nature. :-)

As someone else said, this might better be termed "magicless conduct"; why 
would a "pragmatist" be willing to descend down through Gehennom, get a magic 
amulet, and then go sacrifice it to a god?  And how would a pragmatist 
survive Medusa, since he wouldn't believe her gaze attack?

But even magicless conduct would be impossible in some places.  How could you 
get past the Medusa level (coming back up) without magic?  How would you get 
across the Planes of Air or Fire, or survive the Plane of Water, without some 
magic?  There's probably a few other situations where you need to use a ring, 
amulet, potion, spell or boots to complete the game.

There's already an illiteracy conduct which covers scrolls and spellbooks (and 
fortune cookies), so why not make similar conducts that will cover the whole 
of magic?  Potionless conduct, ringless conduct, wandless conduct, and such.

